00:00:05.759 --> 00:00:12.230 align:start position:0%
 
is everyone connected  well good afternoon and thank you for  joining us  on the second day of this european  council summit  we will shortly be releasing the  conclusions  on both topics on our website and let me  first give the floor to the president of  the european  [Music]  student topic on the agenda today was  climate change we prepared this  european council we had a lot of  consultations before  and we had the opportunity  all the delegations to express  priorities  concerns legitimate concerts in order to  to take into consideration this process  and the responsibility  of the commission that will put on the  table in the next two tweaks  concrete legislative proposals and we  trust the permission to take  this into account we had important  discussions on different topics  ets effort sharing carbon adjustment  mechanism  the importance to take into  consideration the different starting  points  in the different countries and we if we  affirmed  our common goals which are ambitious  climate neutrality by 2050 but also  the decisions that we have taken in  december last year  uh the uh decreased by  55 percent at least 55 of our emissions  by 2030.  um this is a very complex debate  with many areas which are interconnected  it's important to the global vision it's  also important to take into  consideration  the impact assessment for the different  member states  and you can be certain that we will  continue to work  a lot with the member states with the  commission with the european parliament  this process and in preparation of the  next cup 26  in glasgow we hope it'll be possible for  the european union  to continue to play a leading role in to  be to be a concrete in order to make  to make progress  thank you madam president thank you  thank you very much indeed our primary  focus at this  european council at this point in time  continues to be the fight against the  pandemic  both inside the european union and of  course also outside the european union  in the european union it is undeniable  that we have made  great progress since the beginning of  may  we see cases and hospitalizations on a  downward trend that is good  by the end of the week over 300 million  doses of vaccines will have been  delivered to member states  and in june we expect more than 400  million doses  so we are on track to reach our goal  to have enough doses being delivered  to vaccinate 70 percent of the adult  population  in the european union by the end of july  if we continue like this we have  confidence that we will be able to  safely  reopen our societies so we must now  focus on consolidating this progress  one important element that we've been  discussing is the vaccination of minors  we hope for emma to approve  the biotech pfizer vaccines for 12  to 15 year old children  which will then make the vaccination  possible  another topic that we have been  discussing is of course the possibility  of escape variance  vaccines are showing effectiveness  against the known variants present in  europe that's a good  result but of course we know that escape  variance will remain a public health  risk and we are therefore working  hard on setting up an efficient  monitoring  of escape variants for the european  union  the ecdc is monitoring closely the  circulation of variants  within the eu and it is now important  that it expands its monitoring to  a global scale because this will help  stay us presumably one step ahead  of a virus and adapt our strategies  quickly in case that this  is necessary this is all the more  important as we progress  with reopening our societies and in  particular  as we progress in facilitating freedom  of movement  across the european union and indeed  there is then the eu certificate  that plays a role we are very happy that  the political agreement last week  has taken place it marks a major  milestone in this respect  citizens will now be able to use a  mutually recognized certificate  wherever they travel in the european  union  the it infrastructure is ready  at eu level as of june 1st  and as of mid-june when the regulation  enters into force  member states will be able to connect  life to the system  now member states have the key and the  urgent task  to make sure that the national health  systems are fed  with the information of citizens health  status  so that the certificate can be issued  sounds like a lot of work and indeed it  is still a lot of work  but i'm optimistic that we're getting  there i think the certificate  is a unique opportunity to showcase how  the european union  contributes concretely to people's daily  lives  so we must all contribute to make it  happen  on the global response indeed we have  been discussing  on one hand the vaccine sharing  mechanism  and as you know beginning last week  member states have  started to pledge to donate vaccines  which is all the more timely as we know  that  the serum institute of india has halted  all its export to covers until the end  of the year so donations are desperately  needed  and there's a second good news that our  european industrial partners that is  biotech pfizer and modern and johnson  johnson  have committed to deliver 1.3 billion  doses of vaccines in 2021  at non-profit for low-income countries  and at low-cost  to middle-income countries and finally  indeed  we are working on an initiative to  invest one billion euro from team europe  to develop vaccine manufacturing in  africa  the capacity itself in africa it's a  specific initiative with our  african partners initiative not only for  the production  so to build up the manufacturing  capacities but also for  skilled development for the management  of the supply chain  or for example the necessary regulatory  framework through the african  medicines agency this  initiative also aims at bringing mrna  technologies to africa this is something  which hasn't happened so far but  this is our aim to get there  the second topic we've been discussing  indeed is climate  and uh over the past year we have made  growth grow  great progress in charting our course to  climate neutrality in 2050  and as you know we fixed the inter  intermediary target  of minus at least minus 55 percent  of greenhouse gas emissions till 2030.  now uh we have shown to the european uh  council that we are building a model for  a clean economy  that is also a prosperous economy and  both is doable a clean economy and a  prosperous  economy that is our goal today we have  continued the discussions on the  necessary means to achieve these goals  the goals are clear they are fixed in  the climate law so nobody is disputing  that  anymore now we have to discuss the how  how we are getting there  and the discussion this discussion came  very timely because indeed it is a few  weeks before the commission will present  its package  of proposals so it's both timely and  urgent  that we had this discussion one key  element of the proposals  of the fit455 package  is that we will provide  additional eu level measures along  those alongside those of the member  states to help them  to deliver on the targets so this whole  package will consist of 12  different proposals it will combine on  one hand the  carbon pricing what we know as emission  trading system  the ets that is in place it has proven  to be effective it works and on the  other side  it has the targets and the regulatory  standards for example represented  in the effort sharing regulation  important  is that all these different uh  co2 pricing the regulatory framework the  targets  are closely interlinked they are  interlinked  and they have all the same goal that on  one hand we produce  emissions on the other hand we drive  green  innovation and we foster the necessary  investments  to achieve these goals what we want is  sustainable  clean growth and jobs  in this overall framework we have  discussed the two main  components it is the ets indeed and it  is the complementary  effort sharing regulation i have  listened  very carefully to member states views  about how we do this effectively  and fairly some of these measures may  have  social impacts and therefore it is  important from the very beginning  that we think of models  that we are able to support member  states to ensure  a proper social compensation the one  goes with the other  in some we have our goals fixed now and  agreed as i said the climate  law we are now with this regulatory  framework putting in place  all the necessary measures that we need  to reach  these goals and we have mobilized the  necessary  resources the funding to make it work  through the mff  and next generation eu i think this will  boost public and private investment  into the green transition both  investments for  people and for technology thanks  thank you madam president we are first  going to take a question  by uh angela morrow from the huffington  post angela  are you with us thanks marin for the  floor i have two questions one is on  climate  and the question is uh did you have  more expectations because as far as i  understand this  uh summit uh ends with no indications  for the commission for the package that  you're going to present on 14th of july  if you can

